comment_data.body:
It seems to be a combination of `deno lsp` not great response and how 'mini.completion' decides what part before cursor to treat as the "base" for filtering completions.

The setup:
- The '~/.config/nvim-repro/init.lua' file:

    ```lua
    -- Clone latest 'mini.nvim' (requires Git CLI installed)
    vim.cmd('echo "Installing `mini.nvim`" | redraw')
    local mini_path = vim.fn.stdpath("data") .. "/site/pack/deps/start/mini.nvim"
    local clone_cmd = { "git", "clone", "--depth=1", "https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim", mini_path }
    vim.fn.system(clone_cmd)
    vim.cmd('echo "`mini.nvim` is installed" | redraw')

    -- Make sure 'mini.nvim' is available
    vim.cmd("packadd mini.nvim")
    require("mini.deps").setup()

    MiniDeps.add("mason-org/mason.nvim")
    require("mason").setup()

    MiniDeps.add("neovim/nvim-lspconfig")
    vim.lsp.enable({ "denols" })

    require("mini.completion").setup({
      lsp_completion = {
        source_func = "omnifunc",
        auto_setup = true,
      },
    })
    ```

- After initial run with `NVIM_APPNAME=nvim-repro nvim` and all plugins installed, open `:Mason` and install `deno` (find it and press `i`).
- Close and start with `NVIM_APPNAME=nvim-repro nvim -- test.ts`.
- Press `D` and wait until autocompletion. Press `<C-n>` to select `DataView` entry.
- Press `.`. This should autocomplete, but it doesn't.

The reason it doesn't autocomplete is because LSP server responds to `.` with the following result:

```
{
  isIncomplete = false,
  items = { {
      client_id = 1,
      commitCharacters = { ".", ",", ";", "(" },
      data = {
        tsc = {
          name = "apply",
          position = 9,
          uri = "file:///home/evgeni/repos/neovim/test.ts",
          useCodeSnippet = false
        }
      },
      kind = 2,
      label = "apply",
      sortText = "11"
    }, 
    -- More items as the first one
   {
      client_id = 1,
      commitCharacters = { ".", ",", ";", "(" },
      data = {
        tsc = {
          name = "Symbol",
          position = 9,
          uri = "file:///home/evgeni/repos/neovim/test.ts",
          useCodeSnippet = false
        }
      },
      filterText = "[Symbol]",
      insertText = "[Symbol]",
      kind = 6,
      label = "Symbol",
      sortText = "11",
      textEdit = {
        insert = <table 1>,
        newText = "[Symbol]",
        replace = {
          ["end"] = {
            character = 9,
            line = 0
          },
          start = {
            character = 8,
            line = 0
          }
        }
      }
    }, 
    -- More items
  }
}
```

While the first and other similar items don't bring troubles, the "Symbol" one says that the typed dot should be replaced with literal `[Symbol]`. Based on that, 'mini.completion' infers that "." string is what server thinks should be used to filter candidates (i.e. it is "base" of the completion). As default `config.lsp_completion.process_items(items, base)` just checks that new text should start with base, all are filtered out and nothing is shown.

My initial reaction is that it is an issue with the server. But it is also a limitation of the built-in completion: it can only have a single `base` to filter against, while LSP server assumes two here (empty string "" and dot "."). I'll take a closer look at how this can be mitigated, but I don't have high hopes.

---

To extra confirm that this is an issue, try the following setup that doesn't have initial filtersort of LSP response:

```lua
local default_process_items = require("mini.completion").default_process_items
local process_items = function(items, base)
	return default_process_items(items, base, { filtersort = "none" })
end
require("mini.completion").setup({
	lsp_completion = {
		source_func = "omnifunc",
		auto_setup = true,
		process_items = process_items,
	},
})
```
